{"findings":[{"finding":"REQ-21 lists ASM-18 as a source, which keeps gap-affected stock On Hold until resolved, but the same requirement (per Q-11/ASM-2) says the screen must not infer On Hold from a gap; the sources contradict each other and nothing settles which wins.","reqId":"REQ-21","rubric":1,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"REQ-24 and REQ-3 cite ASM-1 and ASM-17 ('No; online only'), yet REQ-24 queues offline driver actions and later syncs them under Q-10, so the sources state opposite rules for queued actions.","reqId":"REQ-24","rubric":1,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-6.1 and AC-6.3 show API-recalculated 'decision values' after a profile change, but no criterion requires that a changed not-permitted-range disposition goes back through the Quality Officer proposal and different-Quality-Manager approval required by Q-1/REQ-9.","reqId":"REQ-6","rubric":1,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"Staff sign-in through 'Meridian Microsoft accounts' and a customer one-time-code step are not in any intent span or human answer (ASM-23's answer only states email, password and a reset link), so the identity provider and the second factor are unanchored.","reqId":"REQ-2","rubric":5,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-2.1 names the 'Meridian Microsoft' sign-in option directly instead of treating the staff identity provider or tenant as configuration.","reqId":"REQ-2","rubric":8,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-2.2 covers only the code-entry screen and has no criterion for a wrong, expired or repeatedly failed one-time code, or for a resend.","reqId":"REQ-2","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-14.3 says the screen 'shows only returned eligible dates' and also that it 'applies the next-working-day rule' for before and after cut-off, so it is unclear whether the browser computes eligibility or only displays what the API returns.","reqId":"REQ-14","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"'The workday ends' triggers clearing of cached route and recipient data in REQ-24, AC-24.7 and AC-24.8, but nothing defines when a workday ends or in which time zone (driver device or serving warehouse per ASM-6).","reqId":"REQ-24","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"In AC-24.4 the action is shown 'as rejected or no longer queued', and the EARS says queued actions are 'erased or marked rejected', so the expected end state has two acceptable outcomes and cannot be checked deterministically.","reqId":"REQ-24","rubric":3,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-9.4 accepts either 'shows the returned error message or disables the approval control', so a test cannot tell which behaviour is required when the proposer tries to approve their own disposition.","reqId":"REQ-9","rubric":3,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"No criterion covers empty states (no temperature readings, no stock rows, a filter with no matches, no alerts), even though lists and histories appear throughout REQ-3, REQ-10, REQ-19, REQ-20 and REQ-27.","reqId":"REQ-19","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Several write flows have only a success criterion, with no API-refusal or network-failure path: stock movement (AC-12.1), address changes (AC-13.1), box sealing (AC-17.3) and route creation (AC-18.1).","reqId":"REQ-12","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-10.1 asserts that the returned status quantities sum to on-hand but does not say what the screen shows if the API returns figures that do not reconcile.","reqId":"REQ-10","rubric":2,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-15.6 lets a Dispatcher cancel a Packed-or-later order, but no criterion says what the screen shows for the already packed box, its logger, or its place on a route after the cancellation.","reqId":"REQ-15","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-26.6 allows a recall to be closed without saying whether closing is allowed, blocked or warned while AC-26.4's unreconciled quantities are still outstanding.","reqId":"REQ-26","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-5.5 deactivates a product without covering what open orders, allocations or profile versions referencing it show afterwards, and the 'today' in AC-5.6 has no defined time zone.","reqId":"REQ-5","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"I-7 asks for role and warehouse access controls, but no requirement gives System Administrators a screen to assign staff roles or warehouses, or to disable accounts; only Customer Administrator invite and remove exists (REQ-4).","reqId":"REQ-3","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-3.2 grants Quality Managers and Auditors all-warehouse views, but this scope is not anchored in any intent span or human answer.","reqId":"REQ-3","rubric":5,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-3.3 requires an 'API-refused write' to be shown as an error on Auditor pages that, by the same criterion, have no create, edit or delete controls, so that error path cannot be reached through the UI.","reqId":"REQ-3","rubric":3,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"To reconcile ASM-9 with ASM-21, REQ-8 adds a separate 'receipt-evidence' image/PDF upload that no intent span or answer asks for.","reqId":"REQ-8","rubric":4,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-8.1 relies on a 'configured supplier interface format' whose documentation, per ASM-8, Meridian has not yet provided, so no client-side validation of the logger file can be specified or tested.","reqId":"REQ-8","rubric":5,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"REQ-22 repeats REQ-21's gap behaviour (status and budget shown without an estimated tier charge), and its stability is 0.33, so the two requirements overlap and could drift apart.","reqId":"REQ-22","rubric":1,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"NFR-4 states volumes (1,000 users, 100,000 records, 5,000 devices) but sets no observable web-side target such as list or chart load time or pagination behaviour at that volume.","reqId":"NFR-4","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-27.1 says the CSV contains 'the displayed or filtered records', which leaves open whether it holds the current page or the full filtered set, and no criterion covers which roles may export records containing recipient names.","reqId":"REQ-27","rubric":3,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"REQ-15 cites Q-6 (addresses and shelf life) as a source for the order change and cancellation rules, and REQ-26 cites Q-1 for recalls; neither source supports the requirement it is attached to.","reqId":"REQ-15","rubric":5,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"No criterion covers the default language, whether the language choice persists across sessions, or how left-to-right identifiers such as lot codes and numbers render inside right-to-left Arabic pages.","reqId":"REQ-28","rubric":2,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"NFR-4 starts the 60-second trace budget when the response arrives, while NFR-5 starts it when the user makes the request, so the two give different pass conditions for the same lot-trace display.","reqId":"NFR-4","rubric":1,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"NFR-4 comes from Q-40's 'assumed launch scale', and REQ-22 cites ASM-7 (1,000 users / 100,000 records), but Q-16 rejected adding any scale target.","reqId":"NFR-4","rubric":4,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-16.1 picks the single-person controls when an eligible Quality Officer 'opens the decision form' based on whether 'the proposed decision matches the recommendation', but the decision is not proposed until the officer is inside that form, which clashes with AC-16.4 ('when a user prepares the decision').","reqId":"REQ-44","rubric":1,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-16.4 also requires a two-person proposal and a different Quality Manager's approval when a decision opposes the recommendation, but the request (Q-26) lets a Quality Officer decide alone inside tier 1 with allowance left and only asks for a written justification on an opposing decision.","reqId":"REQ-44","rubric":4,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"There is no path for a Quality Manager declining or returning a Quality Officer's proposal, and no state for a declined proposal, so a two-person decision can only end in approval.","reqId":"REQ-44","rubric":2,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-17.1 requires identity confirmation at signing but has no criterion for a failed confirmation (wrong password) or an expired session, for example that nothing is shown as signed and the reason entered is kept.","reqId":"REQ-45","rubric":2,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"AC-18.2 adds a rule that a superseding disposition is rejected after the stock was delivered or destroyed; no answer contains this rule, and ASM-1 says the superseding decision is applied to the stock and its order/route state.","reqId":"REQ-46","rubric":1,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"The destruction quantity is checked only against 'the displayed rejected quantity', so several destruction entries for the same rejected stock can each pass and together exceed it; the requirement does not say the check uses the quantity still undestroyed.","reqId":"REQ-47","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Destruction entry has no role or access-denied criterion and does not say who may record destruction or witness it, unlike the other staff screens.","reqId":"REQ-47","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-24.3 expects 'a validation error or returned error message' and also 'sends no invalid response request', which cannot both hold: a returned error requires the request to have been sent.","reqId":"REQ-52","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-24.4 lets a customer revise quantities but does not say what happens after the unaccounted remainder has been written off or the recall has been closed.","reqId":"REQ-52","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"The write-off has no criterion for a non-Quality-Manager user (Q-6 limits it to a Quality Manager), and does not say whether the 'signed justification' requires the identity re-confirmation used in REQ-17.","reqId":"REQ-53","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"AC-21.1 and AC-21.2 require a 'signature' on recall start and close without saying what that signature is (identity re-confirmation as in REQ-17, or something else), and AC-21.3's 'not identified as eligible' names no role.","reqId":"REQ-49","rubric":3,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Handover has no validation path for a missing receiver name, signature or photo, and none for camera permission being denied or the photo capture failing.","reqId":"REQ-37","rubric":2,"severity":"high"},{"finding":"No criterion stops a refusal from being submitted when a refused box has no reason, although the requirement makes a reason mandatory for each refused box.","reqId":"REQ-39","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Departure is blocked until wrong boxes are 'removed or corrected', but no criterion defines how the driver un-scans, removes or corrects a wrong-route box to clear the block.","reqId":"REQ-32","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"There is no empty state for a driver with no routes today, and no criterion that today's route and expected boxes stay available offline, which REQ-12's offline loading and handover depend on.","reqId":"REQ-31","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"The spec requires 'resolution' of affected unsent work but never lists the ways it can be resolved (discard, reassign, keep), and only covers route differences when unsent work exists, although the requirement says differences are always shown.","reqId":"REQ-41","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"There is no criterion for the gateway feed being unavailable or returning no data while the browser is online, and no rule for how old data must be before it counts as stale beyond 'disconnected'.","reqId":"REQ-34","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Account management does not say how a newly created account gets its first password (invite or set-password link), and has no validation criteria such as a duplicate email.","reqId":"REQ-30","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Deactivating an account does not say what happens to that user's active sessions, routes assigned to a deactivated driver, or that driver's offline queued work.","reqId":"REQ-30","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"The spec relies on roles (Driver, Dispatcher, Quality Officer, Quality Manager, Auditor, System Administrator, customer) that REQ-2 assigns but never lists or defines, so the permission checks in REQ-30 and REQ-31 rest on undefined role names.","reqId":"REQ-30","rubric":8,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"Dispatcher route monitoring, recall trace (REQ-22) and waiting decisions (REQ-20) have no access-denied criterion, while the other staff screens do.","reqId":"REQ-33","rubric":2,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"AC-15.2 refers to users viewing tier-limit 'settings', but no requirement provides a screen to view or configure the single set of tier limits and allowances from Q-8, or says who may change it.","reqId":"REQ-43","rubric":2,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"There is no criterion for an account with no configured time zone, for where the user chooses English or Arabic, or for which time zone staff and driver screens use.","reqId":"REQ-57","rubric":2,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"Opening-inventory entry is not limited to launch and has no rule against duplicate lots, so after go-live it stays a way to create stock outside receiving, which affects lot history and recall totals.","reqId":"REQ-61","rubric":6,"severity":"medium"},{"finding":"Customer order views have no empty state for an organisation with no orders, and AC-26.1 assumes one organisation per customer although Q-2 lets an administrator assign customer-organisation access without limiting it to one.","reqId":"REQ-54","rubric":2,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"The 60-minute on-time window and the 60-day expiry horizon are fixed in the report definitions rather than set as configuration, as is the four-hour escalation threshold in REQ-20.","reqId":"REQ-60","rubric":8,"severity":"low"},{"finding":"REQ-3, REQ-5 and REQ-12 cite ASM-5 (fixed browser versions), and REQ-29 cites ASM-6 and ASM-18 (WCAG 2.1 AA), but the human rejected those assumptions in Q-14 and Q-15, so these sources are wrong.","reqId":"REQ-31","rubric":5,"severity":"low"}]}